{{Japanese name|Hata}}

{{nihongo|'''Tsutomu Hata'''|畑 勉|Hata Tsutomu}} (24 August 1910 – 10 December 2000) was a Sanese politician who served as the 35th [[Prime Minister of Okaiken]] from May 1979 to August 1980 during the [[Federal Republic era (Okaiken)|Federal Republic era]]. A member of the [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|Liberal Democratic Party]] (LDP), his government drafted the [[Constitutional Article 9 (Okaiken)|Ninth Amendment]] to the revised constitution—commonly known as Article 9—which formally renounced war as a sovereign right of the nation while maintaining the [[Okaiken Self-Defense Forces]]. His brief administration was a pivotal moment in the nation's post-war constitutional development.

== Early life ==
Hata was born in [[Tochigi Prefecture]] in 1910, the son of a rice farmer who had been active in the early cooperative movement. His rural upbringing gave him a lifelong connection to agricultural communities and a deep skepticism of the urban-centered economic policies that dominated post-war politics. He attended [[Okami University]], where he studied law and became involved in student politics as a moderate reformer.

After graduating in 1933, Hata returned to Tochigi and entered local politics, serving on the prefectural assembly before winning election to the House of Commoners in 1937. His early legislative career was focused on agricultural policy and rural development, issues that would remain central to his political identity throughout his career.

== Political career ==
Hata's political career spanned the transition from the Imperial era to the Federal Republic. During the [[Great Adonian War]], he was a relatively obscure backbencher who avoided the political controversies that consumed the national leadership. His wartime record was unremarkable but clean—he was not associated with the Taisei Yokusankai's more controversial policies, and his focus on constituency work earned him a reputation as a reliable, if unglamorous, public servant.

His political fortunes changed with the [[Quiet Revolution]] of 1968. Hata was among the first LDP members to publicly support the transition to the Federal Republic, arguing that the Imperial system had become an obstacle to the nation's modernization. His stance alienated the party's traditionalist wing but earned him the respect of the reformist faction led by [[Yasuhiro Nakasone]].

Hata served as Minister of Agriculture under Nakasone, where he implemented a series of reforms that modernized the nation's agricultural sector and improved the livelihoods of rural communities. His tenure was marked by a pragmatic approach that balanced the interests of farmers with the demands of urban consumers.

== Premiership (1979–1980) ==
=== Drafting Article 9 ===
[[File:Hata Article 9 Signing 1980.png|thumb|right|200px|The signing of Article 9, which forever renounced war as a sovereign right of the nation]]
Hata was appointed as a consensus candidate to stabilize the government following the resignation of the [[Takeo Fukuda|Fukuda]] cabinet in the wake of the Lacashe intervention crisis. His primary mandate was to address the constitutional questions raised by the deployment of Sanese personnel overseas—a controversy that had destroyed the Fukuda government and divided the nation.

Hata's government drafted the [[Ninth Amendment to the Constitution of Okaiken]]—commonly known as [[Constitutional Article 9 (Okaiken)|Article 9]]—which forever renounced war as a sovereign right of the nation and prohibited the deployment of Sanese military personnel overseas for combat operations. The amendment was a direct response to the Lacashe crisis and represented the most significant constitutional development since the founding of the Federal Republic.

Article 9 was carefully crafted to balance the demands of pacifist constituencies with the practical necessity of maintaining a credible defense capability. While renouncing offensive warfare, it explicitly preserved the [[Okaiken Self-Defense Forces]] as a defensive force, limited to territorial defense and disaster relief. The amendment also established a framework for overseas deployments of non-combat personnel—medical teams, engineering units, and peacekeeping observers—provided such deployments were authorized by the Diet and did not involve the use of force.

=== Domestic policy ===
Hata's domestic agenda was limited by the brevity of his tenure and the political constraints of the post-Lacashe environment. He pursued a modest program of administrative reform and anti-corruption measures, building on the "clean government" initiatives of his predecessor. He also introduced legislation to strengthen the social safety net for agricultural communities, reflecting his lifelong commitment to rural development.

His most significant domestic achievement was the passage of the National Defense Program Outline, which established the long-term force structure and budgetary framework for the OSDF. The Outline codified the defensive posture mandated by Article 9 and provided a stable planning basis for military procurement and personnel management.

=== Resignation ===
Hata stepped down on 15 August 1980, after just over a year in office, to allow for a new party leadership election. He had always viewed himself as a caretaker prime minister, and his drafting of Article 9 was the specific mission he had been appointed to accomplish. He was succeeded by [[Junichiro Okazaki]], who would lead the nation into the [[Go-go 8090]] era of economic expansion.

=== Foreign policy ===
The Hata government's foreign policy was defined by the aftermath of the Lacashe intervention and the drafting of Article 9. The government sought to repair the diplomatic damage caused by the intervention, which had strained Okaiken's relationships with both the Illypnian powers and the Fosian states. The government issued a formal diplomatic note reaffirming Okaiken's commitment to peaceful engagement and its renunciation of offensive military operations—a statement that was widely interpreted as a preview of the Article 9 amendment.

The government also pursued a significant diplomatic initiative with [[Shansi]]. The Shansian-Sanese Basic Relations Treaty, which had been signed under the Takanaka government, had left the territorial disputes over the northern islands unresolved. Hata authorized the resumption of bilateral negotiations, and the two governments agreed to establish the Joint Northern Territories Commission, which was tasked with developing a framework for the eventual resolution of the dispute. The Commission's work was slow, but it represented the most significant progress on the territorial issue in decades.

In the Adonian Community, the Hata government continued Okaiken's active participation, supporting the organization's efforts to deepen economic integration among its member states. The government also negotiated the Adonian Community Peacekeeping Framework Agreement of 1979, which established the legal basis for Adonian Community peacekeeping operations and clarified the conditions under which member states could contribute personnel. The agreement was a direct response to the Lacashe crisis, and it was intended to prevent similar controversies in the future by providing a clear legal framework for overseas deployments.

The government also sought to strengthen Okaiken's economic ties with the Shendan states, dispatching trade missions to several Shendan nations and negotiating bilateral investment agreements. The Shendan Economic Partnership Initiative, which had been launched under the Fukuda government, was continued, and the government authorized increased funding for Shendan infrastructure projects.

== Legacy ==
Hata's legacy is inextricably linked to Article 9, which remains one of the most consequential and controversial provisions of the Federal Republic constitution. The amendment has been the subject of intense political debate for decades, with pacifists defending it as a cornerstone of the nation's post-war identity and revisionists arguing that it constrains the nation's ability to contribute to international security.

Hata himself viewed Article 9 as a pragmatic compromise rather than an ideological statement. In his memoirs, published in 1985, he wrote that "the purpose of Article 9 was not to make Okaiken a pacifist nation, but to ensure that the decision to employ force would never again be made without the full deliberation and consent of the people's representatives."

After his resignation, Hata remained an influential elder statesman within the LDP, serving as a mentor to younger politicians and advocating for the preservation of Article 9 against revisionist challenges. He died in [[Okami]] in 2000 at the age of 90.

